# Git Workflow

A git workflow for any change that will be reviewed. Sloppy history is unreviewable and unrevertable; these habits keep it legible.

## 1. Start fresh

Branch from an up-to-date `main`. A branch cut from stale `main` carries conflicts you did not write.

```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
git status   # clean tree before branching
```

## 2. Branch with intent

```bash
git checkout -b feature/<slug>
git checkout -b fix/<slug>
git checkout -b chore/<slug>
```

The name states the type and the intent. A reader should know what the branch does without reading the diff.

## 3. Commit in logical units

One idea per commit. If the message needs "and," it is two commits.

```bash
git add -p              # split a dirty tree into logical hunks
git diff --staged       # read it before committing
git commit -m "feat(scope): imperative description"
```

Atomic commits are what make a branch revertable.

## 4. Write conventional-commit messages

`type(scope): description`. Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`.

- Imperative mood, no trailing period, under ~72 characters.
- Add a body after a blank line explaining why, not what.

## 5. Push and open a reviewable PR

```bash
git push -u origin feature/<slug>
```

A reviewable PR does one thing, stays small, and has a description stating what changed, why, and how it was verified.

## 6. Keep it reviewable

Answer review feedback with new commits. Keep the branch current with `main` so the diff stays honest. Resolve conflicts on your branch, not the reviewer's.
